Slide 1 — Decisions That Survive the Meeting

0:00–0:40

[Look into the camera. Smile.]

“Hi everyone, I’m Bhavya. Have you ever finished a meeting, felt like everyone agreed, and thought, ‘Great, now we can move forward’?”

[Pause 2 seconds.]

“Then later, you realize people left with different ideas of what that decision meant.”

“That’s what I want to talk about today: how do we make a decision that still holds after the meeting ends? I’ll share a small habit I’ve started using in the last few minutes of a meeting.”

[Click to slide 2. Give people a second to see it.]

Slide 2 — The meeting ended. The decision didn’t hold.

0:40–1:55

“Picture a meeting where we agree to roll something out in phases. Everybody nods. It sounds settled, and the call ends.”

[Pause.]

“But one person thinks phase one means starting with a small group. Someone else thinks it means a much broader rollout. We all said ‘yes,’ but we didn’t confirm exactly what we said yes to.”

[Glance briefly at the steps on the slide, then look back into the camera.]

“You can see the pattern here: we agree, we leave with different plans, and a week later we’re having the same discussion again.”

[Pause 2 seconds.]

“That’s why I started paying attention to how we end a meeting. We may spend the whole hour discussing a topic, but if we rush the last few minutes, the next step can still be unclear.”

[Click to slide 3.]

Slide 3 — Say it. Pin it. Own it.

1:55–2:55

“My reminder is simple: Say it. Pin it. Own it.”

[Leave a small pause between each phrase.]

“Say it: I read the decision back in one plain sentence, so people can tell me if I’ve missed something.

Pin it: I write it where the group can see it, so we have one version to work from.

Own it: We agree on one person and one date for the next step before we leave.”

[Look into the camera.]

“These are small actions, but they’re easier to do while everyone who made the decision is still together.”

[Pause.]

“So, what would that actually sound like?”

[Click to slide 4.]

Slide 4 — The four lines that make the close real

2:55–4:30

“Let me use a simple example. Suppose we’re discussing how to introduce API automation to the CMS team. Instead of ending with ‘Sounds good,’ I’d slow us down and say:”

[Speak the next four lines as though you’re closing that meeting. Don’t rush them.]

“‘So our decision is to start with a small, agreed set of API tests, then expand from there. Did I get that right?’”

[Pause 2 seconds.]

“‘Who owns confirming that first set of tests?’”

[Pause 2 seconds.]

“‘By when? And what do we do if that date slips?’”

[Pause 2 seconds.]

“‘Does anyone see a concern with this plan that we should discuss before we leave?’”

[Pause 3 seconds. Look into the camera.]

“That final question matters to me. Someone may see a dependency or a sequencing problem but hasn’t found a moment to say it. This gives them that moment while we can still work through it together.”

[Click to slide 5.]

Slide 5 — Where the decision goes after the room

4:30–5:30

“Now we’ve said the decision aloud, agreed on an owner and date, and checked for concerns. There’s one last step: record it.”

[Glance briefly at “Record” on the slide.]

“I put it in the meeting notes or the tracker the team already uses. It should be clear enough that someone who wasn’t in the room can understand what happens next.”

“For that API automation example, the record would say what the first set of tests includes, who will confirm it, the agreed date, and any concern that came up.”

[Pause.]

“When we meet again, we can ask, ‘What happened with that action?’ We don’t have to spend the first part of the meeting trying to remember what we decided.”

[Click to slide 6.]

Slide 6 — When I used it for real

5:30–7:10

[Look into the camera. Tell this part like your own experience.]

“This became important to me in our API automation work. We had a kickoff where a phased rollout received a clear ‘yes.’ At the time, it felt like we were aligned.”

[Pause.]

“But afterward, people were working toward different versions of the plan. It made me realize that agreeing on the direction wasn’t enough. We needed to make the immediate next step clear too.”

“So I began closing those discussions differently. I’d read the decision back, write it where everyone could see it, and confirm one owner and a date.”

[Pause.]

“I brought that same approach into a discussion with the CMS team. When I asked for concerns before we closed, a sequencing risk came up while we were still together. We could talk about it with the right people in the room.”

[Pause 2 seconds.]

“That showed me why those last few minutes matter. They help us leave with the same understanding and a next action we can actually follow up on.”

[Click to slide 7.]

Slide 7 — What I’m taking with me

7:10–8:00

[Look into the camera. Slow down and bring the audience back to your opening.]

“Remember that meeting where everyone nodded, but people left with different plans?”

[Pause.]

“Now picture a different ending. Before anyone leaves, we say the decision out loud. We hear any concerns. We write it down, with one owner and one date.”

[Pause. Look into the camera.]

“The next time we meet, we’re not asking, ‘What did we decide?’ We’re asking, ‘How is it going?’”

[Pause 2 seconds.]

“That’s a decision that survives the meeting. Thank you.”

[Stop. Let the ending land. Then say:]

“I’m happy to take questions.”
